{0 camlcast}

A first-person raycasting engine in OCaml on top of SDL2 ([tsdl]).

It began as a Wolfenstein-style grid raycaster and grew past it. A
{!Raycaster.World} is a graph of independently-authored {!Raycaster.Room}s, each
in its own coordinates, joined at named doorways that you both see and walk
through: an open one is a portal, drawn recursively into the opening, and a
{!Raycaster.Transform} carries the camera and the ray across. Rooms are built
from wall {e segments} at any angle, not cells of a grid; the floor is an
inclined {!Raycaster.Plane} and the ceiling — per room — is an inclined plane or
open {!Raycaster.Sky}; walls carry their own heights and {!Raycaster.Material}s,
can be see-through, and can be hung with decals; {!Raycaster.Image} sprites stand
in the world facing the player; and there is mouse look with pitch. Each frame is
drawn into a CPU pixel buffer: the floor, ceiling and sky are cast per pixel, the
opaque walls (with their decals) are painted over them, then the sprites, then
the see-through walls last so the room behind shows through. Surfaces take their
patterns from a procedural {!Raycaster.Texture} and their colour from their
material, dimmed by the world's {!Raycaster.Atmosphere}. The buffer is then
scaled to the window by the GPU.

{1 Two libraries}

The engine holds no content: not one colour, pattern, picture or room. What it
has instead are the types those things are values of, so a game supplies its own
and two games can share an engine without sharing a look.

- {b [camlcast.raycaster]} — the engine. Geometry, ray casting, rendering, SDL.
- {b [camlcast.demo]} — the showcase level and the art it is made of, runnable as
  [camlcast-demo]. It is content, and it lives outside the library it is content
  for; it stays here because it exercises every corner of the engine at once.

{1 Building a game on it}

{!Raycaster.Engine.run} takes a {!Raycaster.World} and an optional
[grow : World.t -> Player.t -> World.t], which it calls whenever the player
crosses into another room. That one function is the whole interface: the engine
hands back whatever it was drawing and takes whatever it is given, so any state
a game needs beyond the world — a catalogue, a random seed, what it has already
built — stays on the game's side of the line and the engine stays a pure
function of the world it is handed.

{{:https://github.com/pharick/house}The House} is the game built this way: a
roguelike of endless liminal rooms that grows itself as the player walks,
keeping every room within {!Raycaster.Config.max_portal_depth} doorways of them
finished — exactly as deep as the renderer looks.

{1 Where to start}

{!Raycaster.Ray} carries the ray-versus-segment derivation and why the distance
it reports is free of fish-eye. {!Raycaster.Plane} carries the one-line equation
that casts the sloped floor and ceiling per pixel. {!Raycaster.Viewport} ties
them to the screen: the projection, the eye height, the pitch shear, and — since
the window is resizable and can go fullscreen at any frame — the rules that
decide what a resize does. {!Raycaster.Renderer} puts it all together.
{!Raycaster.World} carries both the portal machinery and the three primitives a
world grows by.

{1 The engine}

Each depends only on the ones above it.

- {!Raycaster.Config} — every tunable constant, in one place
- {!Raycaster.Result_ext} — [let*] and a short-circuiting range loop, for
  chaining fallible SDL calls
- {!Raycaster.Vec} — immutable 2-D vectors, with the dot and cross products the
  geometry needs
- {!Raycaster.Transform} — rigid motions between room-local coordinate frames
- {!Raycaster.Plane} — an inclined floor/ceiling plane and its casting equation
- {!Raycaster.Color} — 8-bit RGB, shading and blending
- {!Raycaster.Texture} — the machinery for procedural greyscale patterns, and the
  wrapping value noise they are built from
- {!Raycaster.Material} — what a surface is made of: a colour and a pattern
- {!Raycaster.Atmosphere} — the air a world is seen through: its fog, its haze,
  and where its light comes from
- {!Raycaster.Sky} — the open sky where a room has no roof
- {!Raycaster.Image} — full-colour images with alpha, for decals and sprites
- {!Raycaster.Room} — one room's walls, thresholds, surfaces, sprites and
  collision
- {!Raycaster.World} — named rooms, linked portals, the world's air and spawn,
  and how a world grows
- {!Raycaster.Ray} — ray-versus-segment intersection: every wall the ray
  crosses, farthest first
- {!Raycaster.Player} — the camera, as position + unit [dir] + unit [right],
  plus the pitch it looks up and down by
- {!Raycaster.Viewport} — window size to camera geometry; the eye height, the
  pitch shear and the resize rules
- {!Raycaster.Input} — SDL keyboard and mouse-look, as engine intent
- {!Raycaster.Framebuffer} — a CPU pixel buffer and the streaming texture it
  uploads through
- {!Raycaster.Renderer} — the software renderer: casts the planes, paints the
  walls, per pixel
- {!Raycaster.Engine} — window lifetime, fullscreen state, and the game loop

{1 The demo}

- {!Camlcast_demo.Patterns} — masonry, joinery, tile and glazing
- {!Camlcast_demo.Pictures} — two decals and two sprites
- {!Camlcast_demo.Surfaces} — the demo's materials, its daylight sky and its air
- {!Camlcast_demo.Level} — the five-room showcase world
